{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"6f1f899ef3523d5c","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is Amazon VPC?**|What is Amazon VPC?]]","x":-100,"y":0,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"33a410ff25d1fbe5","type":"text","text":"`Amazon VPC` means `Amazon Virtual Private Cloud`.\nAmazon VPC lets you create your own private, controlled, and secure network inside AWS.  \nNormally, if you run servers in a company data center, you need to design the network yourself.\nFor example:\n```\nIP addresses\nSubnets\nRouting\nGateways\nSecurity\n```\nAmazon VPC gives you the same kind of control, but inside the AWS Cloud.\n**Simple idea**  \nA `VPC` is like your own private network inside AWS.  \nInside that network, you can run resources like:\n```\nEC2 servers\nDatabases\nLoad balancers\nContainers\n```\nYou control how those resources connect to each other and to the internet.  ","x":350,"y":0,"width":600,"height":630},
		{"id":"0ce43d7215ebb5a7","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a VPC CIDR block?**|What is a VPC CIDR block?]]","x":-100,"y":690,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"1e954d6041d512e4","type":"text","text":"When you create a VPC, you choose an IP address range.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis range is called a `CIDR block`.  \nThe CIDR block decides what private IP addresses can exist inside your VPC.  ","x":350,"y":690,"width":600,"height":215},
		{"id":"4dc24fffaaacd286","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What does `10.0.0.0/16` mean?**|What does `10.0.0.0/16` mean?]]","x":-100,"y":965,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"56b4f1dd71bfc69a","type":"text","text":"```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis means your VPC can use IP addresses from the `10.0.x.x` range.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.0.1.10\n10.0.2.25\n10.0.100.50\n```\nA `/16` CIDR block gives a large number of IP addresses.  ","x":350,"y":965,"width":600,"height":330},
		{"id":"cbbbd193856d5609","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**Can you change the main VPC CIDR block later?**|Can you change the main VPC CIDR block later?]]","x":-100,"y":1355,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"3589b670da4373a0","type":"text","text":"After you create the VPC, the main CIDR block cannot be changed.  \nSo you should choose it carefully.  \nFor example, if you create your VPC with:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThat becomes the primary IP range for the VPC.  ","x":350,"y":1355,"width":600,"height":215},
		{"id":"bf4b04300b4d678b","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a subnet?**|What is a subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":1630,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"4b518f485a0aecd0","type":"text","text":"A `subnet` is a smaller network inside a VPC.  \nYou divide a VPC into subnets to organize resources.  \nFor example, you may create:\n```\nPublic subnet\nPrivate subnet\n```\nA public subnet can be used for web servers.  \nA private subnet can be used for databases.  \n**Example VPC and subnet design**  \nYour VPC may use:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThen you divide it into smaller subnets:\n```\n10.0.1.0/24    Public subnet\n10.0.2.0/24    Private subnet\n```\nThe VPC is the big network.  \nThe subnets are smaller parts inside that network.  ","x":350,"y":1630,"width":600,"height":585},
		{"id":"1b44182e397e0d97","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a public subnet?**|What is a public subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":2275,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"092edb2d357129c2","type":"text","text":"A `public subnet` is a subnet that can connect to the internet through an `Internet Gateway`.  \nYou usually put internet-facing resources here.  \nFor example:\n```\nWeb server\nLoad balancer\nBastion host\n```","x":350,"y":2275,"width":600,"height":250},
		{"id":"a6e8eeacbf1671af","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a private subnet?**|What is a private subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":2585,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d240d723e8d27f3d","type":"text","text":"A `private subnet` is a subnet that is not directly reachable from the public internet.  \nYou usually put internal resources here.  \nFor example:\n```\nDatabase\nPrivate backend service\nInternal application server\n```\nPrivate resources can still access the internet through a `NAT Gateway`, but the internet cannot directly access them.  ","x":350,"y":2585,"width":600,"height":275},
		{"id":"29ed184b4b1b82b2","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**Does a VPC belong to one AWS Region?**|Does a VPC belong to one AWS Region?]]","x":-100,"y":2920,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"a85379a80e5f888f","type":"text","text":"Yes.  \nA VPC belongs to one AWS Region.  \nFor example, if you create a VPC in the Singapore Region, it exists only in that Region.  \nYou can create multiple VPCs in the same Region.  ","x":350,"y":2920,"width":600,"height":135},
		{"id":"5e1335923cf42c96","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What does CIDR block size mean?**|What does CIDR block size mean?]]","x":-100,"y":3115,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"406dea59429d5b28","type":"text","text":"The CIDR block size controls how many IP addresses your VPC can have.  \nFor example:\n```\n/16 means 65,536 IP addresses\n/28 means 16 IP addresses\n```\nA smaller number after `/` means a bigger network.  \nA bigger number after `/` means a smaller network.  ","x":350,"y":3115,"width":600,"height":245},
		{"id":"c491b89c012d9f3f","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**Why should VPC CIDR blocks not overlap?**|Why should VPC CIDR blocks not overlap?]]","x":-100,"y":3420,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"12dc39b245119895","type":"text","text":"Your VPC CIDR block should not overlap with other networks.  \nThis is very important when you connect your VPC to another network.  \nFor example:\n```\nCompany office network\nAWS VPC\nVPN network\nAnother VPC\n```\nIf the IP ranges overlap, routing becomes confusing.  \n**Example of overlapping CIDR problem**  \nImagine your company network uses:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThen your AWS VPC should not also use:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nBecause when traffic wants to go to:\n```\n10.0.1.20\n```\nThe system becomes confused.\n```\nIs 10.0.1.20 in the office network\\?**\nOr is 10.0.1.20 inside the AWS VPC\\?**\n```\nSo you should choose a different CIDR block for your VPC.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.10.0.0/16\n```\nor:\n```\n172.16.0.0/16\n```","x":350,"y":3420,"width":600,"height":1015},
		{"id":"54a0eceb39404a80","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What came before Amazon VPC?**|What came before Amazon VPC?]]","x":-100,"y":4495,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"3181eabefd722e0d","type":"text","text":"Amazon VPC came after Amazon EC2.  \nIn the past, AWS had two networking models:\n```\nEC2-Classic\nEC2-VPC\n```","x":350,"y":4495,"width":600,"height":195},
		{"id":"9c81fa19bdd14844","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What was EC2-Classic?**|What was EC2-Classic?]]","x":-100,"y":4750,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"7febf58f59aa9c9b","type":"text","text":"`EC2-Classic` was the old AWS networking model.  \nIn this model, resources used a shared, flat network.  \nThat means EC2 instances were not running inside your own private VPC.  \nMany AWS customers used the shared AWS network model.  ","x":350,"y":4750,"width":600,"height":135},
		{"id":"67a8b3ed05d306af","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is EC2-VPC?**|What is EC2-VPC?]]","x":-100,"y":4945,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"899c8663f7bb4c73","type":"text","text":"`EC2-VPC` is the current AWS networking model.  \nIn this model, resources run inside isolated virtual networks.  \nThat virtual network is your VPC.  \nThis is more secure and gives you more control.  ","x":350,"y":4945,"width":600,"height":135},
		{"id":"51594361aad37864","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is the default VPC?**|What is the default VPC?]]","x":-100,"y":5140,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"97d3342870ba5dfa","type":"text","text":"AWS creates a default VPC in each Region.  \nThe default VPC uses this CIDR block:\n```\n172.31.0.0/16\n```\nThe default VPC is useful for learning and quick testing.  \n**Should you use the default VPC for production?**  \nFor real production systems, it is usually better to create a custom VPC.  \nA custom VPC gives you better control over:\n```\nIP ranges\nSubnets\nRouting\nGateways\nSecurity\nNetwork architecture\n```\n**Important VPC components**  \nThere are four important VPC components:\n```\nSubnet\nRoute table\nInternet Gateway\nNAT Gateway\n```\nThese components help control how traffic moves inside and outside your VPC.  ","x":350,"y":5140,"width":600,"height":740},
		{"id":"2a37d4db5ebd2a29","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a subnet?**|What is a subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":5940,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"eb4f2f3f96de615a","type":"text","text":"A `subnet` is a smaller network inside a VPC.  \nYou use subnets to organize resources.  \nFor example:\n```\nPublic subnet for web servers\nPrivate subnet for databases\n```","x":350,"y":5940,"width":600,"height":220},
		{"id":"3ef0c8c20f91c8f6","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a route table?**|What is a route table?]]","x":-100,"y":6220,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"2334b4144858390d","type":"text","text":"A `route table` is a set of rules that tells network traffic where to go.  \nFor example, a route table can say:\n```\nTraffic to the internet → Internet Gateway\nTraffic inside VPC → local route\nTraffic to private network → VPN Gateway\n```\nRoute tables control network paths.  ","x":350,"y":6220,"width":600,"height":250},
		{"id":"69a371432990b677","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a gateway?**|What is a gateway?]]","x":-100,"y":6530,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"63f670590be9aa90","type":"text","text":"A `gateway` is a connection point between networks.  \nIn AWS, gateways help your VPC connect to:\n```\nThe internet\nAnother private network\nAn on-premises network\n```","x":350,"y":6530,"width":600,"height":225},
		{"id":"18de13d50834bf0a","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is an Internet Gateway?**|What is an Internet Gateway?]]","x":-100,"y":6815,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"1101edc22b0613c0","type":"text","text":"An `Internet Gateway` is a VPC component that allows resources in public subnets to access the internet.  \nIt also allows internet traffic to reach public resources in your VPC.  \nFor example, a public EC2 web server needs an Internet Gateway to be reachable from the internet.  ","x":350,"y":6815,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"f95172c214fd7acd","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a NAT Gateway?**|What is a NAT Gateway?]]","x":-100,"y":6995,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"64a97af265bbb277","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Gateway` allows private resources to access the internet without allowing the internet to directly access them.  \nFor example, an EC2 instance in a private subnet may need to download software updates.  \nIt can use a NAT Gateway for outbound internet access.  \nBut people from the internet cannot directly connect to that private EC2 instance.  ","x":350,"y":6995,"width":600,"height":135},
		{"id":"f9583bf835f7ac16","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a Virtual Private Gateway?**|What is a Virtual Private Gateway?]]","x":-100,"y":7190,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"f83cf7317d8fbe51","type":"text","text":"`Virtual Private Gateway`, or `VGW`, is a gateway used to connect a VPC to another private network.  \nIt is often used with a VPN connection to connect AWS to an on-premises network.  \nFor example:\n```\nCompany office network → VPN → Virtual Private Gateway → AWS VPC\n```","x":350,"y":7190,"width":600,"height":190},
		{"id":"1aca5ef61bf76a22","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is IPv4?**|What is IPv4?]]","x":-100,"y":7440,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"fe3c2c33e5aaed0a","type":"text","text":"`IPv4` is the older and most common IP address system.  \nExample IPv4 address:\n```\n192.168.1.1\n```\nMost common private network ranges use IPv4.  ","x":350,"y":7440,"width":600,"height":190},
		{"id":"df9beb7b3d00bdda","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is IPv6?**|What is IPv6?]]","x":-100,"y":7690,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"297b8eac7ff2576b","type":"text","text":"`IPv6` is a newer IP address system with a much larger address space.  \nIPv6 addresses are longer than IPv4 addresses.  ","x":350,"y":7690,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"72620cd114a135c5","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a primary CIDR block?**|What is a primary CIDR block?]]","x":-100,"y":7870,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"5ec20c7bffd9cd80","type":"text","text":"A `primary CIDR block` is the main IP address range assigned to a VPC when it is created.  \nExample:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis is the main private IP range for the VPC.  ","x":350,"y":7870,"width":600,"height":190},
		{"id":"95c51c7b3ae8371f","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a CIDR block?**|What is a CIDR block?]]","x":-100,"y":8120,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"8a22f6eb8058aaad","type":"text","text":"A `CIDR block` is an IP address range.  \nIt can be used by:\n```\nVPC\nSubnet\nNetwork\n```\nExample:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nor:\n```\n10.0.1.0/24\n```\n<!--SR:!2026-07-30,1,230-->","x":350,"y":8120,"width":600,"height":460},
		{"id":"1c7261a227726784","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is an on-premises network?**|What is an on-premises network?]]","x":-100,"y":8640,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"cf83cf15be3697a8","type":"text","text":"An `on-premises network` is a company's own physical network outside the cloud.  \nFor example, your company may have:\n```\nOffice building\n├── Routers\n├── Switches\n├── Servers\n└── Computers\n```\nThis network is managed by the company, not by AWS.  ","x":350,"y":8640,"width":600,"height":310},
		{"id":"1894a527708b922d","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is Amazon EC2?**|What is Amazon EC2?]]","x":-100,"y":9010,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d3047896bf5cae67","type":"text","text":"`Amazon EC2` is a service that lets you create and run virtual servers in AWS.  \nAn EC2 instance is like a server in the cloud.  \nYou can install applications, databases, or backend services on it.  ","x":350,"y":9010,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"77004a45b18251f3","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a flat network?**|What is a flat network?]]","x":-100,"y":9190,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"0b3285fc1c8525a8","type":"text","text":"A `flat network` is a simple network without strong separation between different parts.  \nIn a flat network, many resources may exist in the same network area.  \nThis gives less control compared to a properly designed VPC with subnets and routing.  ","x":350,"y":9190,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"3fd94acb3698903a","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a shared network?**|What is a shared network?]]","x":-100,"y":9370,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"1c12add5ac9d0806","type":"text","text":"A `shared network` is a network used by many customers or systems together.  \nEC2-Classic used a shared network model in the past.  \nVPC is better because it gives each customer isolated network control.  ","x":350,"y":9370,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"5765443ef1350b4c","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is subnet sizing?**|What is subnet sizing?]]","x":-100,"y":9550,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"1952dc1a2cd7881f","type":"text","text":"`Subnet sizing` means choosing how large a subnet should be.  \nYou choose the subnet size by selecting a CIDR block.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.0.1.0/24\n```\nA `/24` subnet gives 256 IP addresses.  \nYou should choose subnet sizes carefully so you have enough IP addresses for your resources.  ","x":350,"y":9550,"width":600,"height":240},
		{"id":"b2edb5b77ca813f7","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is external internet traffic?**|What is external internet traffic?]]","x":-100,"y":9850,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"6b5538f5241fe450","type":"text","text":"`External internet traffic` is traffic coming from outside AWS.  \nFor example:\n```\nUser browser → Internet → Your public web server in AWS\n```\nThis is inbound traffic from the public internet.  ","x":350,"y":9850,"width":600,"height":190},
		{"id":"8c2fd36ec55188a0","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is internet-bound traffic?**|What is internet-bound traffic?]]","x":-100,"y":10100,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"64bc8789cdfd4492","type":"text","text":"`Internet-bound traffic` is traffic going to the internet.  \nFor example:\n```\nPrivate EC2 instance → NAT Gateway → Internet\n```\nThis is outbound traffic from your VPC to the internet.  ","x":350,"y":10100,"width":600,"height":190},
		{"id":"ee3802145cde7ec2","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is network architecture?**|What is network architecture?]]","x":-100,"y":10350,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"624579ecdb71ed59","type":"text","text":"`Network architecture` is the overall design of how your network is organized.  \nIt includes:\n```\nVPCs\nSubnets\nRoute tables\nGateways\nSecurity groups\nCIDR blocks\nTraffic flow\n```\nGood network architecture makes your system more secure and easier to manage.  ","x":350,"y":10350,"width":600,"height":370},
		{"id":"937442c22a97ad2c","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a VPC component?**|What is a VPC component?]]","x":-100,"y":10780,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"2a3d86632440f277","type":"text","text":"A `VPC component` is a networking part inside or attached to a VPC.  \nExamples:\n```\nSubnet\nRoute table\nInternet Gateway\nNAT Gateway\nSecurity group\nNetwork ACL\n```","x":350,"y":10780,"width":600,"height":315},
		{"id":"50c706713e9daf56","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a private IP address?**|What is a private IP address?]]","x":-100,"y":11155,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"c0e1b4ef967f3ed3","type":"text","text":"A `private IP address` is an internal IP address used inside a private network.  \nExample:\n```\n10.0.1.20\n```\nPrivate IP addresses are used for communication inside the VPC.  \nThey are not directly reachable from the public internet.  ","x":350,"y":11155,"width":600,"height":215},
		{"id":"b8b75f53b5e902f8","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a public IP address?**|What is a public IP address?]]","x":-100,"y":11430,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"27f84edba2b046c9","type":"text","text":"A `public IP address` is an IP address that can be used on the public internet.  \nIf an EC2 instance has a public IP address and the correct routing/security settings, people from the internet can reach it.  ","x":350,"y":11430,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"48d2cd3dd22e8045","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is an Elastic IP address?**|What is an Elastic IP address?]]","x":-100,"y":11610,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"672aaf5e2907ddda","type":"text","text":"An `Elastic IP address` is a static public IP address that you can allocate in AWS and attach to resources.  \nUnlike a normal public IP address, an Elastic IP can stay the same.  \nFor example, you can attach it to an EC2 instance or a NAT Gateway.\n<!--SR:!2026-07-30,1,230-->","x":350,"y":11610,"width":600,"height":135},
		{"id":"47350061e8a84481","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is source IP?**|What is source IP?]]","x":-100,"y":11805,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"abb3e869b486b223","type":"text","text":"`Source IP` is the IP address where traffic comes from.  \nFor example:\n```\nUser IP → Web server\n```\nThe user's IP address is the source IP.  ","x":350,"y":11805,"width":600,"height":190},
		{"id":"a4ceeed2b1281cf8","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is Network Address Translation?**|What is Network Address Translation?]]","x":-100,"y":12055,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"848ddbb72596409f","type":"text","text":"`Network Address Translation`, or `NAT`, means changing one IP address into another IP address when traffic moves between networks.  \nFor example, a private EC2 instance may use a NAT Gateway to access the internet.  \nThe internet sees the NAT Gateway's public IP address, not the private EC2 instance's private IP address.  ","x":350,"y":12055,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"e1704213972bf7bc","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is outbound traffic?**|What is outbound traffic?]]","x":-100,"y":12235,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"a9f0a4c9b9f12f82","type":"text","text":"`Outbound traffic` is traffic going out from your VPC to the internet or another network.  \nExample:\n```\nEC2 instance → Internet\n```","x":350,"y":12235,"width":600,"height":165},
		{"id":"c86180096bdb3dbe","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is inbound traffic?**|What is inbound traffic?]]","x":-100,"y":12460,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"7213dd396773dd8b","type":"text","text":"`Inbound traffic` is traffic coming into your VPC from the internet or another network.  \nExample:\n```\nInternet user → Public web server\n```","x":350,"y":12460,"width":600,"height":165},
		{"id":"923fe8381878c168","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What are data transfer rates?**|What are data transfer rates?]]","x":-100,"y":12685,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"68928af970b4927d","type":"text","text":"`Data transfer rates` are costs charged based on how much data moves in or out.  \nFor example, AWS may charge for data transfer from your VPC to the internet.  ","x":350,"y":12685,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"9db8f75e29dbebbd","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What are bandwidth constraints?**|What are bandwidth constraints?]]","x":-100,"y":12865,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"02e9a371dfcecb5e","type":"text","text":"`Bandwidth constraints` are limits on how much network traffic can pass through.  \nFor example, some resources or network paths may have limits on traffic speed or volume.  ","x":350,"y":12865,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"4f85b6c23cfe3ce2","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a NAT Instance?**|What is a NAT Instance?]]","x":-100,"y":13045,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"3c473a11afa08067","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Instance` is an EC2 instance manually configured to perform NAT.  \nYou manage it yourself.  \nCompared with a NAT Gateway, a NAT Instance gives more control but requires more maintenance.  ","x":350,"y":13045,"width":600,"height":120},
		{"id":"d991f2de82715b22","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a protocol?**|What is a protocol?]]","x":-100,"y":13225,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"f41dd347a5f73638","type":"text","text":"A `protocol` is a communication method.  \nExamples:\n```\nHTTP\nHTTPS\nTCP\nUDP\nICMP\nSSH\n```\nProtocols define how computers communicate with each other.  \n**Simple VPC example**  \nA common VPC design looks like this:\n```\nVPC: 10.0.0.0/16\n├── Public subnet: 10.0.1.0/24\n│   ├── Web server\n│   └── Load balancer\n│\n├── Private subnet: 10.0.2.0/24\n│   ├── Backend server\n│   └── Database\n│\n├── Internet Gateway\n│   └── Allows public subnet to access the internet\n│\n└── NAT Gateway\n    └── Allows private subnet to access the internet\n```\n**Simple idea**  \nA `VPC` is your private network inside AWS.  \nA `CIDR block` is the IP range of the VPC.  \nA `subnet` is a smaller network inside the VPC.  \nA `route table` controls where traffic goes.  \nAn `Internet Gateway` lets public resources connect to the internet.  \nA `NAT Gateway` lets private resources access the internet safely.  \nFor production, you usually create a custom VPC instead of only using the default VPC.  ","x":350,"y":13225,"width":600,"height":1060}
	],
